      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing the logarithms in a form that allows us to equate the arguments directly.\n\nFirst, we need to understand the logarithmic expressions \\(\\log_x 4\\) and \\(\\log_{27} 3\\). By the definition of logarithms, we can rewrite these as:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\log_{\\text{some base}} 4 = \\frac{\\log_2 4}{\\log_2 x}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\log_{\\text{some base}} 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{\\log_2 27}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have \\(\\log_2 27 = \\log_2 (3^3) = 3 \\log_2 3\\). Therefore, we can rewrite the second logarithm as:\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 3}{3 \\log_2 3} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute this back into the original equation:\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\log_2 4}{\\log_2 x} = \\frac{2}{\\log_2 x}.\n\\]\nWe are given that \\(\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\\), so we can set the two expressions for \\(\\log_x 4\\) equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{\\log_2 x} = \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\nBy solving for \\(\\log_2 x\\), we get:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3 = \\log_2 x \\implies \\log_2 x = 6.\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\nx = 2^6 = 64.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
