      "text": "To solve for \\( x \\) in the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing both logarithms in a more usable form. First, we convert \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) to base 3. Using the change of base formula for logarithms, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_3 3}{\\log_3 27}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\log_3 3 = 1\\) and \\(\\log_3 27 = \\log_3 (3^3) = 3\\). Therefore,\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis equation tells us that \\(x^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 4\\). To solve for \\(x\\), we raise both sides to the power of 3:\n\n\\[\nx = 4^3 = 64\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).",
