      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing both logarithms in terms of the same base. We can use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that \\(\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\\). Using the natural logarithm (or any base logarithm), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_x 4 = \\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 27}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 27}\\), we can rewrite the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} = \\frac{\\ln 3}{\\ln 27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(\\ln 27\\). Using the property of logarithms \\(\\ln (a^b) = b \\ln a\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\ln 27 = \\ln (3^3) = 3 \\ln 3\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\ln 27 = 3 \\ln 3\\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} = \\frac{\\ln 3}{3 \\ln 3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln 3\\) in the denominator and numerator cancel out, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\ln 4}{\\ln x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n3 \\ln 4 = \\ln x\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms \\(\\ln a^b = b \\ln a\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\ln x = \\ln 4^3 = \\ln 64\n\\]\n\nThus, we find:\n\n\\[\nx = 64\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{64}\n\\]",
