      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\log_x 4 = \\log_{27} 3\\), we start by expressing the logarithms in terms of base 10 or natural logarithms (ln), but since the base of the logarithm is not explicitly specified, we assume the common logarithm (base 10). Thus, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 4 = \\log_{27} 3\n\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) in terms of base 10. Recall that \\(27 = 3^3\\), so we can rewrite \\(\\log_{27} 3\\) as:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{\\log_{10} 27}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(27 = 3^3\\), we have \\(\\log_{10} 27 = \\log_{10} (3^3) = 3 \\log_{10} 3\\). Substituting this into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{27} 3 = \\frac{\\log_{10} 3}{3 \\log_{10} 3} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute this back into the original equation:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10} 4 = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten in exponential form as:\n\n\\[\nx = 4^{1/3} = \\sqrt[3]{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt[3]{4}}\n\\]",
